Let's find $\dfrac{dy}{dx}$. 

$\begin{align}
\dfrac{dy}{dx}&=\dfrac{\left( \dfrac{dy}{d\theta} \right)}{\left( \dfrac{dx}{d\theta} \right)}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{2\theta\sin(\theta)+\theta^2\cos(\theta)}{2\theta\cos(\theta)-\theta^2\sin(\theta)}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{2\sin(\theta)+\theta\cos(\theta)}{2\cos(\theta)-\theta\sin(\theta)}
\end{align}$ 


(**Note**: Since we are not worried about $\theta = 0$ in this particular problem, we can divide through by $\theta$ to simplify.) 

